15:28:45 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I completely understand why you'd feel that way—your own experiment seems to show that nothing happens until 100°C. But let’s take a closer look at what might have been happening in that experiment, and why it can appear that way even though evaporation *is* occurring. ### Key point: Your measurement may not have been sensitive enough In a typical classroom demo, you might heat a beaker of water over a Bunsen burner or hot plate. The water level is often measured by eye, perhaps with a ruler or a mark on the glass. Let's do some quick math: - A typical beaker might hold 200 mL of water with a surface area of about 50 cm². - At 20°C, water evaporates at roughly 0.1 grams per hour per square centimeter (depending on humidity and airflow). That's about **5 grams per hour** for that beaker. - Over the 5–10 minutes it takes to heat the water from 20°C to 100°C, that's only about **0.4 to 0.8 grams** of water lost. - That's less than 1 mL of volume—about the size of a few drops. Can you see that change in a 200 mL beaker by eye? Almost certainly not. Now, when the water reaches 100°C and starts boiling, the rate of water loss skyrockets. Boiling can remove water at a rate of **tens of grams per minute**. So suddenly the water level drops dramatically and you can see it. **Your eyes didn't deceive you—the pre-boiling loss was just too small to see.** ### But wait—could the experiment have had a flaw? Sometimes in school experiments, the beaker is covered or the water is heated so quickly that the small amount of evaporation before boiling is masked by condensation on the cooler sides of the beaker. Water vapor from early evaporation might have condensed on the glass above the water line, so it never left the system. That would make the water level seem unchanged. Only when boiling starts does the vapor escape forcefully enough to leave the beaker. ### A simple test you can do at home - Fill two identical glasses with the same amount of water. - Leave one on the counter, uncovered, for **one week**. - Leave the other in the fridge (also uncovered) for the same week. - After a week, compare the water levels. You will see that both have dropped—the one at room temperature more than the fridge one. That is evaporation at work, at temperatures far below boiling. No vapor cloud, no bubbles, just a slow, invisible loss. ### The bottom line Your experimental observation is valid: **before boiling, the water level change was too small to notice with your eyes over such a short time.** But that doesn't mean no evaporation occurred. The same process that makes puddles dry up on a cool day was happening in your beaker—just at a rate too slow to see. I encourage you to try the week-long test. It's the most convincing way to show that water really does "disappear" without boiling. Let me know what you find!
